      "writtenOn": "2023-11-20T21:19:21Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "Doing the cubic clamping here interacts weirdly if we have an alpha swizzle that would take values from the R channel. I\u0027m not sure what the best solution is, or if we should also be pushing all color space conversion into each sample?\n\nDoes Ganesh do cubic filtering, and then convert to the dst color space? Or does it convert each sample and then filter? Presumably HW filtering is doing the filtering in the source color space, so graphite\u0027s behavior is consistent with that.",
      "range": {
        "startLine": 299,
        "startChar": 0,
        "endLine": 300,
        "endChar": 56
      },
      "revId": "951b9056476c75c7e3de9b192e65e3a9fdc1a1d4",
      "serverId": "5086850b-e599-37d2-8ec8-ff16aeccbf02"
    },
    {
      "unresolved": true,
      "key": {
        "uuid": "73fc8c53_362d9b56",
        "filename": "src/sksl/sksl_graphite_frag.sksl",
        "patchSetId": 3
      },
      "lineNbr": 300,
      "author": {
        "id": 5065
      },
      "writtenOn": "2023-11-21T15:35:59Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "We don\u0027t use \"rrrr\" at all -- I\u0027m not sure why we have it. In the \"000r\" case, we are sampling from a single-component texture, so essentially we start with R001. So the alpha values should be equal across all samples and unless something is really odd with the coefficients we should end up with 1, and the RGB clamp should be ok. I suppose we could end up with .99 for A but I\u0027d still expect the final R to be less than or equal to that.",
      "parentUuid": "ece6b868_e5ac5c07",
      "range": {
        "startLine": 299,
        "startChar": 0,
        "endLine": 300,
        "endChar": 56
      },
